{
    "word": "morepork",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "6f00b768-213c-3f20-95bc-ce5ecfcd5bc0",
                "word": "morepork",
                "pos": "intj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "8e25afc9-feae-3351-9833-88d635da029a",
                        "entry_id": "6f00b768-213c-3f20-95bc-ce5ecfcd5bc0",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The call of a morepork."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c28a29d2-4f12-48b8-ae22-c32ed9628a37",
                                "sense_id": "8e25afc9-feae-3351-9833-88d635da029a",
                                "text": "[T]he morepork, a species of caprimulgus, or goatsucker,^([sic]) keeps up all night the exact repetition of its own name, \"Morepork! morepork!\" It is a sound to me most expressive of a dreary loneliness and twilight distance that I ever heard."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4e2dd36d-d47d-4fce-8f5a-4f4df0e32fb7",
                                "sense_id": "8e25afc9-feae-3351-9833-88d635da029a",
                                "text": "As soon as the shades of evening close in over the Australian forest, the ear is startled by the cry of \"morepoke,\" clearly and loudly repeated, and a bird as large as an owl flits by on noiseless wing, like the goat-sucker at home. This is the Morepoke, a species of large night-jar,^([sic]) all head and mouth, about the size of an owl."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e5c2edc9-f754-47fe-b2b1-db03267266df",
                                "sense_id": "8e25afc9-feae-3351-9833-88d635da029a",
                                "text": "As soon as night begins to spread its darkening shadows over our brief twilight, this active creature sets out on its hunting excursions, roaming over field and gully with soft, noiseless flight, one of the advantages it owes to its flocculent plumage; its cry of \"morepork, morepork,\" is repeated at frequent intervals, sometimes with the utmost rapidity of utterance; [...]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6934b8b5-f175-4757-8f4e-666280ef2dce",
                                "sense_id": "8e25afc9-feae-3351-9833-88d635da029a",
                                "text": "‘I assure you. I do rather a good morepork.’ Bernard sat up, straightened his shoulders. ‘More pork. More pork.’"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e0497668-344a-4b56-8c54-d6a776fdcadd",
                                "sense_id": "8e25afc9-feae-3351-9833-88d635da029a",
                                "text": "I know where a morepork lives, in the roof of our dunny, and I hear it hoot ‘morepork, morepork’ every night. And I hoot ‘morepork’ back to it, and it hoots ‘morepork’ back to me."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
                "entry_id": "058ffda3-78a8-3b25-9401-03e77c3ebf84",
                "word": "morepork",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "b8923ee4-0f38-4192-be83-fcf7605c134c",
                        "entry_id": "058ffda3-78a8-3b25-9401-03e77c3ebf84",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "moreporks"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1770a356-8c96-3d13-810e-19559387e457",
                        "entry_id": "058ffda3-78a8-3b25-9401-03e77c3ebf84",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The Tasmanian spotted owl (Ninox novaeseelandiae), a small brown owl indigenous to New Zealand and Tasmania."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "New-Zealand"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "b0d6e7e1-dd00-46fe-a715-05f5ac97e11c",
                                "sense_id": "1770a356-8c96-3d13-810e-19559387e457",
                                "text": "[T]he eye of the Morepork or Greater Night Jarr,^([sic]) which I lately had the opportunity of examining, is wonderfully adapted for enabling it to see the insects in the dark, on which it feeds."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "542ad7af-128a-4913-b0bc-fc4d19da72d6",
                                "sense_id": "1770a356-8c96-3d13-810e-19559387e457",
                                "text": "Although the living figure of our owl may be unknown to most persons, from the nature of its habits, yet few, perhaps very few, of the older settlers are unacquainted with the human-like cry of the More-pork."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f1a95319-fb18-4b43-b6e8-b063307a2d1d",
                                "sense_id": "1770a356-8c96-3d13-810e-19559387e457",
                                "text": "[I]t was better fun wandering about with the old man at night than moping in my hut, listening to the morepoke."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "643d77e8-b586-405b-9b03-993a6ea429c8",
                                "sense_id": "1770a356-8c96-3d13-810e-19559387e457",
                                "text": "When Heke and Kawiti were making an attack on the Europeans in the Bay of Islands, the native parties, in taking up their positions before daybreak, communicated their movements to one another by imitating the cry of the morepork, which the sentries were accustomed to hear, and of which they therefore took no notice."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "22e35f45-6ecb-4459-83d3-c47eaf4af310",
                                "sense_id": "1770a356-8c96-3d13-810e-19559387e457",
                                "text": "Moreporks and laughing owls were both recorded from the Punakaiki karst in small numbers, although the presence of laughing owls was inferred only from prey remains. [...] Because moreporks were extremely rare in fossil deposits, Millener (1991) suggested that they were recent colonists. In Punakaiki, one of the morepork records is of bones beneath an entrance tomo."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ed6b1407-8c85-4727-bbd5-eb78482e6ee9",
                                "sense_id": "1770a356-8c96-3d13-810e-19559387e457",
                                "text": "I remember that at night there was usually a morepork in the tree, whose eerie calls scared me as I lay still in bed, with firmly closed eyes, waiting for a possible attack."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d9f87b03-7cef-4278-9420-2de67a3a7c5d",
                                "sense_id": "1770a356-8c96-3d13-810e-19559387e457",
                                "text": "[T]wo New Zealand Moreporks (Ninox novaeseelandiae) were introduced to Norfolk Island in 1987, where only one individual of the closely related Norfolk Island Boobook (Ninox novaeseelandiae undulata) survived [...]. Nest boxes were erected across the island to supplement the dearth of natural hollows. By early 1995, the population had risen to 11 birds [...]."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a17eb805-27d2-48e3-b0c5-e9e68d10e47c",
                                "sense_id": "1770a356-8c96-3d13-810e-19559387e457",
                                "text": "I heard the harsh rasping of a weta, a mega-cricket. Then a pair of Morepork owls began a duet. Few birds are as aptly named as the Morepork. From dusk to sun-up and from forest to suburban garden, this medium-sized owl emphatically calls for \"more pork.\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "32546044-7f12-433c-bab4-0fc6d3c523c9",
                                "sense_id": "1770a356-8c96-3d13-810e-19559387e457",
                                "text": "Moreporks’ inquisitive, searching eyes, / scan the hiding places, search the ground, / it stares ’round … from flattened rounded face."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b1f016db-b8cf-4bfd-9d4e-71c230c6c036",
                                "sense_id": "1770a356-8c96-3d13-810e-19559387e457",
                                "text": "We were a family of odd bods and every summer we spent by the river. At night the morepokes called again and again."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9dacbad2-a27a-37a6-8b69-ee9f8065f209",
                        "entry_id": "058ffda3-78a8-3b25-9401-03e77c3ebf84",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Used as a mild term of abuse.",
                            "A foolish person; also, a person who is wearisome."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Australia",
                            "New-Zealand",
                            "colloquial",
                            "derogatory"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "cff77c65-2ca4-459c-9f4d-b31561d6c745",
                                "sense_id": "9dacbad2-a27a-37a6-8b69-ee9f8065f209",
                                "text": "It is interesting that the Opposition morepork is starting to chirp. [...] We have all heard about the \"gang of four and a half\"—the morepork brigade."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "63518a79-02a6-32ea-a32a-29d006062ac5",
                        "entry_id": "058ffda3-78a8-3b25-9401-03e77c3ebf84",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Used as a mild term of abuse.",
                            "A person who is lazy or slow; a slowcoach, a slowpoke."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Australia",
                            "New-Zealand",
                            "colloquial",
                            "derogatory"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "175094ec-3487-468c-b6f4-4d1cd2dc009c",
                                "sense_id": "63518a79-02a6-32ea-a32a-29d006062ac5",
                                "text": "Where's that old morepoke? O, you're there, are you? Fetch the jack off o' your wagon—come! fly roun'! you're (very) slow for a young fellow."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
